<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Prince-Bishopric of Naumburg</h1><p class="st-meta">1028 CE – 1564 CE · capital Zeitz</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An Ottonian bishopric on the Saale famed for its cathedral's lifelike founder statues, absorbed into Saxony after the Reformation.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The bishopric was founded at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeitz">Zeitz</a> in 968 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a>. In 1028, with the approval of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-john-xix">Pope John XIX</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conrad-ii-holy-roman-emperor">Emperor Conrad II</a>, it moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naumburg">Naumburg</a>, a new seat founded by the Ekkehardine margraves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> ruled lands around Naumburg and Zeitz.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naumburg-cathedral">Naumburg Cathedral's</a> west choir, made around 1250 by the anonymous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naumburg-master">Naumburg Master</a>, holds twelve statues of the cathedral's founders, including the margravine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uta-von-ballenstedt">Uta</a>, among the finest works of Gothic sculpture. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> were imperial princes but leaned heavily on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wettin">Wettin</a> rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1542 the Elector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> installed the Lutheran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-of-amsdorf">Nicholas of Amsdorf</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> against the chapter's choice. After the last Catholic bishop, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-von-pflug">Julius von Pflug</a>, died in 1564, the see came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> administrators. From 1656 to 1718 it formed Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeitz">Zeitz</a>, and in 1815 it passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/prince-bishopric-of-naumburg.jpg" alt="Prince-Bishopric of Naumburg" width="311" height="327"><figcaption>Image: Gustav Droysen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bistum_Merseburg_1250.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1028 CE – 1564 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Zeitz</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Nicholas of Amsdorf, Julius von Pflug</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity, later Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle High German, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Naumburg-Zeitz, Hochstift Naumburg</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1296&amp;focus=prince-bishopric-of-naumburg" aria-label="Prince-Bishopric of Naumburg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4948 405 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Prince-Bishopric of Naumburg lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5268 661L5229 694M5268 661L5312 664"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5312 664h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5268 661h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5268 661h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1296&amp;focus=prince-bishopric-of-naumburg">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
